            "A practical first-pass review can look for several recurring signals: undefined terms, inconsistent terminology, unclear pronouns, uncertain scope, missing actors, open-ended deadlines, conditional duties with no decision-maker, and exceptions that lack limits. These signals do not prove a defect. They direct the reviewer to ask whether the instrument elsewhere supplies a definition or procedure and whether the parties intended the apparent openness.",
            "Scope is often concealed in words such as all, relevant, necessary, or affected. The reviewer should ask what population, territory, activity, or period the word covers and whether that scope changes across provisions. Pronouns such as it, they, or the same may refer to more than one institution or action. A definition section can reduce uncertainty, but a definition that conflicts with ordinary usage or another clause can create a new interpretive question.",
