            "A historical case can illuminate a drafting risk, but it cannot serve as a ready-made blueprint. Before recommending a safeguard, students should identify the specific mechanism they believe mattered in the case and compare it with the conditions of the proposed agreement. A boundary-monitoring mechanism may be relevant to a territorial instrument but not to a constitutional settlement whose central dispute concerns political representation. An escrow arrangement may be useful for phased financial performance and entirely unsuitable for obligations involving fundamental rights.",
            "The recommendation should distinguish a general lesson from a case-specific inference. A general lesson might be that parties benefit from identifying the authority responsible for collecting implementation evidence. A case-specific inference might be that a particular delayed delivery contributed to mistrust in one agreement. The second requires evidence about timing, expectations, and the parties' responses; the first is a design proposition that can be argued on institutional grounds. Blending them can make an example look more conclusive than its record supports.",
            "The analyst should also ask what the safeguard might cost. A monitoring body requires funding and expertise; independent inspections may create security concerns; public reporting can affect negotiations; strict deadlines can disadvantage a party with limited capacity. A safeguard that cannot be staffed or sustained may produce false confidence. The recommendation should name the responsible institution, expected resources, and contingency if the institution cannot perform.",
